* cpu1: where a fast ADC channel rolls off, at both ends of its band.
*
* Measured as a step response rather than a frequency sweep. For a single-pole
* RC the two say the same thing - the time to 63.2 % of a step is RC, and the
* corner is 1/2*pi*RC - and ngspice's .meas can read a transient vector without
* ambiguity where an AC one gives its real part unless coaxed. The identity is
* the only thing taken on trust here, and it is one line of algebra.
*
* One RC, and the impedance the power board is required to present in front of
* it. That promise is part of the filter: a sensor with a hundred ohms of output
* impedance moves this corner by a decade with nothing on this board changing,
* which is why `header.source_impedance` is declared and read here.
*
* `checks/test_adc.py` computes the same corner from the same values. This is
* the second method, and it is worth having only while the two can disagree.
*
* **Two networks, because one could only ever agree.** This deck ran a single
* branch with the source impedance, the series resistor and the shunt capacitor
* all at `:max`, under a comment saying that was "the end that matters: too low
* and the loop measures a current that happened a moment ago". Half of that was
* right. `adc.fast_corner` is a band with two ends, the check holds the network
* to both, and a deck evaluating only the low one cannot contradict the check
* about the high one whatever it finds. Fit a 3.3 nF part instead of the 4.7
* and the single-branch version reported 1.81 MHz, inside the declared 1 to 2,
* and passed - while the check computed 2.46 MHz at the other corner and
* rejected it. The simulation would have signed off a network the design
* refuses.
*
* So: `low` is every value at the end that makes the corner lowest, `high` is
* every value at the end that makes it highest, and both are held against the
* same declared band. They share one source so the two are driven identically.
*
* Values come from the build; @path:end@ is an instance path and which end of
* its resolved range to take.

Vin in 0 PULSE(0 1 0 10p 10p 5u 10u)

* The slowest the channel may be: the most source impedance the power board is
* allowed to present, the largest series resistor, the largest capacitor.
Rsource_low in src_low @header.source_impedance:max@
Rseries_low src_low pin_low @adc.fast1.series.resistance:max@
Cshunt_low pin_low 0 @adc.fast1.shunt.capacitance:max@

* And the fastest: a power board that presents nothing, and both passives at
* the bottom of their tolerance. This is the end that decides whether an
* aliasing filter is still a filter.
Rsource_high in src_high @header.source_impedance:min@
Rseries_high src_high pin_high @adc.fast1.series.resistance:min@
Cshunt_high pin_high 0 @adc.fast1.shunt.capacitance:min@

.tran 0.2n 2u

* The time constant, straight off the step, and the corner that follows from
* it. `t_tau` is banded as well as the corner it produces, because a deck that
* stops converging and returns a time of zero would otherwise report an
* infinite corner and pass nothing but the wrong test.
.meas tran t_tau WHEN v(pin_low)=0.6321206 RISE=1
.meas tran f_corner PARAM='1/(6.283185307*t_tau)'

.meas tran tmp_tau_high WHEN v(pin_high)=0.6321206 RISE=1
.meas tran f_corner_high PARAM='1/(6.283185307*tmp_tau_high)'

* And how long the pin takes to become a faithful copy. The measurement below
* is to a tenth of a per cent, which is ln(1000) = **6.9** time constants, not
* the four and a half this used to say - 4.5 leaves 1.1 % and is the figure for
* a per cent, not a tenth of one. The comment was wrong and the measurement was
* right, which is the way round that goes unnoticed.
*
* Taken on the slow branch, which is the one that bounds how soon a channel may
* be re-read.
.meas tran t_settled WHEN v(pin_low)=0.999 RISE=1

.end
